{
  "requirement": "Pass the `next` UUID exactly as returned by the previous page. The UUID\nidentifies the prepared result and determines the shape of the returned object.\n\n```text\nget_next_page({uuid: UUID}) -> object\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.graph.validation-context.pages.mcp` has three obligations: the call takes `{uuid}`, the UUID is the `next` value passed back exactly as returned, and the UUID picks the prepared result and so the shape of the returned object. The tests cover each of them.\n\n**Passing `next` back unchanged.** Every continuation test copies `next` straight from the previous page into `get_next_page({uuid})`. They use it as returned and assert on the resulting page.\n\n**The UUID selects the prepared result and its shape.** The tests use `get_next_page` across the kinds of prepared result:\n- **Source text:** `fixed_tools_deliver_domain_contents_with_mandatory_snapshot_dependencies` pages a `read_source` result. It checks that each page keeps the same `node` and that the concatenated text equals the original. Repeating a call returns identical text.\n- **Listing:** `listing_continuations_return_complete_paths_from_the_original_listing` follows `next` and gets `{files, next}` pages. Together they equal the original listing even though a file was added mid-way.\n- **Outline:** `outline_continuations_keep_the_file_and_complete_ordered_items` checks that each page has `path` and `items`, and that the items are complete and in order.\n- **Search:** `search_obligations_follow_delivery_and_keep_the_original_snapshot` follows a search page. It checks the shape of the result (`matches` with unique tags, no `instructions`, a fresh `search_id`) and that it is a snapshot taken before the file changed.\n- **Graph node:** `application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation` follows a `read_node` page. It asserts the shape `{text, next}`, that the new `next` differs from the old one, and that it is a valid UUID.\n\n**Bad identifiers.** The same tests assert that an invalid UUID, a nil UUID, and a UUID from another session or conversation all return errors. This shows the UUID, not the tool name, selects the result.\n\n**Tool signature.** `tools.rs` declares `get_next_page` with a required `uuid` string of format uuid. The tests call it only with that argument. They check that the tool is present, but they don't assert the schema text. The schema is a literal pass-through and has no conditional. The requirement's signature, `{uuid}` returning an object, is exercised through every dispatch.\n\nI found no plausible violation that these tests would accept.",
  "references": "267a8604fa87dad28b3a9caae930a2e3841bd342d0d4fc6a81cfc67d09ab0e21",
